What Account A needs to do — 5 steps only
Account A is purely passive — no Lambda, no EC2, no S3, no SNS. It just exposes one IAM role that Account B's Lambda assumes into.

Step 1 — Create IAMGuardianReadOnlyRole (most important step)
This has 3 sub-parts:

Create the role — trusted entity type: "Another AWS account" → enter Sasi's Account B ID
Replace the permissions — don't use the broad ReadOnlyAccess managed policy. Paste the least-privilege inline policy (IAMGuardianReadPolicy) that only allows iam:List*, iam:Get*, and sts:GetCallerIdentity
Tighten the trust policy — the default trusts the entire Account B. Replace it so only Sasi's specific Lambda role ARN can assume it, and add an ExternalId condition (IAMGuardianHackathon2024) to prevent confused deputy attacks

Step 2 — Verify via CloudShell (no EC2 needed)
Run aws sts assume-role from AWS CloudShell in Account A to confirm the role and trust work before handing over to Sasi.
Step 3 — Tell Sasi to add ExternalId
Since you added an ExternalId condition, Sasi must add ExternalId="IAMGuardianHackathon2024" to her Lambda's sts.assume_role() call — otherwise it will be denied.
Step 4 — Send handover checklist to Sasi
Four things: Account A ID, Role ARN, ExternalId value, Region.
Step 5 — Verify via CloudTrail
After Sasi runs her Lambda test, check CloudTrail → Event history → filter AssumeRole to confirm Account B successfully read Account A's IAM data.
